search/Loose+Ends+Hangin%27+On+A+String+%28Frankie+Knuckles+Club+Mix%29+%5BMixed%5D", "search": true}, {"label": "discogs", "url": "https://www.discogs.com/search/?q=Loose+Ends+Hangin%27+On+A+String+%28Frankie+Knuckles+Club+Mix%29+%5BMixed%5D&type=all", "search": true}], "notes": ["2007 Defected"]}, {"t": 6504, "time": "01:48:24", "artist": "Bill Withers", "title": "Who Is He? (And What Is He to You?)", "conf": "high", "links": [{"label": "bandcamp", "url": "https://bandcamp.com/search?q=Bill+Withers+Who+Is+He%3F+%28And+What+Is+He+to+You%3F%29", "search": true}, {"label": "youtube", "url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bill+Withers+Who+Is+He%3F+%28And+What+Is+He+to+You%3F%29", "search": true}, {"label": "soundcloud", "url": "https://soundcloud.com/search?q=Bill+Withers+Who+Is+He%3F+%28And+What+Is+He+to+You%3F%29", "search": true}, {"label": "spotify", "url": "https://open.spotify.com/search/Bill+Withers+Who+Is+He%3F+%28And+What+Is+He+to+You%3F%29", "search": true}, {"label": "apple", "url": "https://music.apple.com/au/song/391725488", "search": false}, {"label": "deezer", "url": "https://www.deezer.com/search/Bill+Withers+Who+Is+He%3F+%28And+What+Is+He+to+You%3F%29", "search": true}, {"label": "discogs", "url": "https://www.discogs.com/search/?q=Bill+Withers+Who+Is+He%3F+%28And+What+Is+He+to+You%3F%29&type=all", "search": true}], "notes": ["1972 Legacy Recordings"]}, {"t": 6644, "time": "01:50:44", "artist": "Kirk Franklin & The Family", "title": "Gonna Be a Lovely Day", "conf": "mid", "links": [{"label": "bandcamp", "url": "https://bandcamp.com/search?q=Kirk+Franklin+%26+The+Family+Gonna+Be+a+Lovely+Day", "search": true}, {"label": "youtube", "url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kirk+Franklin+%26+The+Family+Gonna+Be+a+Lovely+Day", "search": true}, {"label": "soundcloud", "url": "https://soundcloud.com/search?q=Kirk+Franklin+%26+The+Family+Gonna+Be+a+Lovely+Day", "search": true}, {"label": "spotify", "url": "https://open.spotify.com/search/Kirk+Franklin+%26+The+Family+Gonna+Be+a+Lovely+Day", "search": true}, {"label": "apple", "url": "https://music.apple.com/au/song/303131183", "search": false}, {"label": "deezer", "url": "https://www.deezer.com/search/Kirk+Franklin+%26+The+Family+Gonna+Be+a+Lovely+Day", "search": true}, {"label": "discogs", "url": "https://www.discogs.com/search/?q=Kirk+Franklin+%26+The+Family+Gonna+Be+a+Lovely+Day&type=all", "search": true}], "notes": ["1998 Gospocentric"]}, {"t": 6887, "time": "01:54:47", "artist": "Baby'O", "title": "In The Forest", "conf": "mid", "links": [{"label": "bandcamp", "url": "https://bandcamp.com/search?q=Baby%27O+In+The+Forest", "search": true}, {"label": "youtube", "url": "https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baby%27O+In+The+Forest", "search": true}, {"label": "soundcloud", "url": "https://soundcloud.com/search?q=Baby%27O+In+The+Forest", "search": true}, {"label": "spotify", "url": "https://open.spotify.com/search/Baby%27O+In+The+Forest", "search": true}, {"label": "apple", "url": "https://music.apple.com/au/search?term=Baby%27O+In+The+Forest", "search": true}, {"label": "deezer", "url": "https://www.deezer.com/search/Baby%27O+In+The+Forest", "search": true}, {"label": "discogs", "url": "https://www.discogs.com/search/?q=Baby%27O+In+The+Forest&type=all", "search": true}], "notes": []}], "counts": {"high": 23, "mid": 5, "low": 1}, "transcripts": [{"start": "00:05:26", "end": "00:06:08", "text": "Good evening, welcome. Stephen Ferris here, back in Studio 2 I think it's called. We originally started 23 years ago in Studio 1 and shifted to Studio 2 and then we've had a reno and it looks like it's high tech, it's Star Trek, it's 2000 and whatever. Hopefully I won't make a mistake tonight but anyway, FBi, Sold Out, that's the show, Stephen Ferris, that is Rose Royce and that's the Chaz Summers extended remix of that track Still in Love which came out. It's a beautiful track, great piano solo. 1978, I think it was, on the album 3, Rose Royce, 3, Rose Royce being the band, Gwen Dickey being the singer. Hope you enjoy the tunes."}, {"start": "00:14:20", "end": "00:15:28", "text": "That's a nod to the ancestors of Jill Scott there with the lyrics sort of working off Off the Back of My Ancestors. The track is called Off Offer the Back. And she lists both her personal family line, the Brasswell family, as well as musical pioneers like Nina Simone, Ella Fitzgerald, Sarah Vaughan, Frankie Lyman. Wow. Tina Turner, Billie Holiday, amongst many others. And, of course, Philadelphia Queen is sold there. Jill Scott, to whom this may concern, is the brand new album. Came out maybe about six months ago, five months ago, Offer the Back there. And before that, we had Hamilton Bohannon, one of my favourites, from 1981, going for another one. It's the title tune of that album with the same name from the man who originally started out as a drummer at Motown Records and then became an incredible producer, arranging guitar player, singer, just a funk nut, you know. So there's so many tracks you can play from Bohannon, it's not funny. And the very top there, Still in Love, which, not the album cut. The album cut's probably better, but as far as dance floors go, Chaz Summers' extended rework from Rose Royce. Chaka Khan talking of Chaka Khan another single curious subject from the album Chuck Zilla just out"}, {"start": "00:25:02", "end": "00:26:04", "text": "At the very top there, Chaka Khan, 2026. Curious subject, the brand new one from the album, Shaxilla. It's been a gold rush of singles coming off that album. This is Souled Out. Don't forget, of course, under a couple of months away, we're having a party at National Art School, which is where we sort of kicked everything off with a little freebie down at the Darlow Bar, which was a beauty. Rodney O, hello. Josh Beagley, myself, and Rodney O on the microphone did that one. And then we moved over to the National Arsenal for a series of run through the summer months, the warmer months, at the Selbach Theatre, indoors and outdoors from 2 o'clock. But we're going back there on the 8th of November. And we'd love you to come. It's already selling quickly. I'll be doing some giveaways further down the track, but not yet. So we're just going to hold off a little bit longer. And FBI supporters will be in the mix for those as well. But if you want tickets, Flick It is the place to go. And on the particular website, you'll see the poster for this band. Earth, Wind and Fire. Saturday night"}, {"start": "00:35:48", "end": "00:36:39", "text": "Doug Williams sang this particular tune. Love it. That is L-T-D and a track called Every Time I Turn Around. That's in brackets. Back in Love Again. Geoffrey Osborne, the vocalist from the album Something to Love for 1977. And I think probably their biggest single to date at that point. For that also from 77, The Commodores and a song called Brick House. Who doesn't love Brick House? It's featured in many, many funny films over the years. At the very top, The Earth, Wind and Fire. Saturday Night from the Spirit album for 1976. Shifting decades to the 90s This is Teddy Riley's Construct Black Street You Blow My Mind from 1994 That was the debut album called Black Street And this was I think the second single from it You're on Sold Out Stephen Ferris with you till 7pm FBI"}, {"start": "00:46:28", "end": "00:48:32", "text": "Of course, is Raphael Wiggins with his brother Dwayne Wiggins and Timothy Riley who make up Tony, Tony, Tone. And Raphael was the producer of Note When It Came to Keeping Sensibility That Was Old School intact. Annie May, coming back into his life. Sons of Sob. For that 96, we have Blue Boy, Remember Me. Well, you couldn't forget it because it was everywhere. When it was played, it was nearly to the point of annoying. But some great samples, I should say. Marlena Shaw from Women of the Ghetto from her 73 album and a little bit of Detroit Emeralds 1971 so nicely put together by Blue Boy Alexis Blackmore was the man behind Blue Boy in 1996 and it was a really big big hit very tough there for 1994 You Blow My Mind, Blackstreet, Teddy Riley Chauncey Hannibal, Walter Scott Markel Riley and Akil Davidson, Teddy of course being the producer of Note got to the point where he did Michael Jackson's Dangerous, etc., plus lots, lots more, and very, very active on social media if you want to know more about what he does and how he does it. This is Sold Out. That's the show. My name is Stephen Ferris. You are tuned to FBI out of the Beating Heart right here in the corner of the pocket of really Redfern slash Waterloo slash Alexandria. We're right there near the metro and been here since 2003 supporting local Sydney music. This is one of the luxuries. We call this a legacy show because I've been around for so long now that they allow me to do what I do and we celebrate that at our sold out our parties and this next one coming up in November the 8th bear that in mind it's called sold out 8 they're looking for it on Flickett Prince the estate we knew that music would come out it's been very tightly controlled which is a good thing very good thing and some of the tracks have been heard before but this next one comes from the most releasing compilation called Timeless and it's a track called I Am You and sorry it's a track called Calabama And never before heard It was recorded in 2003 And now it's out Prince"}, {"start": "01:01:17", "end": "01:03:21", "text": "It's very hypnotic, isn't it? It's not surprising. Remix by Hot Toddy, who does a lot of great sort of soulful house tracks, and his sense of hypnotism is dragging you in and keeping you impeccable. That track is called Call Girl, the Hot Toddy remix. The Right Now is the band. 2012, the album came out, Gets Over You. And The Right Now, it's a fairly innocuous sort of title, isn't it? Funded by vocalist Stephanie Berej, coming out of Chicago. Well, that we had Birdie, Angela Martin, the vocalist, Something About You. And the very top there we had Unheard Before, originally released in 2003, but now out on the brand new release called Timeless by Prince. Calabama is the tune and is the collection of tunes that perhaps haven't seen the light of day or were lost in the vaults and are now seeing some sunlight. Beautiful stuff. It is now 6.02, tuned to FBI 94.5. Stephen Ferris with you till 7 p.m. here on Sold Out. I was reminded I did a party recently And Talking Heads was the flavour I never got to play this Which I should have done From Brian Eno and David Byrne 1979 to 80 They got together for a collaboration And if ever there was two art school students Creating one of the greatest albums ever It was this one My Life in the Bush of Ghosts And it got delayed because of the sample clearances So it didn't come out until after Remain in Light Now Remain in Light came out I think from 80 Mid 80 my guess I arrived in San Francisco, booked my tickets, went and saw Talking Heads perform it. And of course, that was when they brought in their extended lineup of musicians to create what was that sort of cross-polyrhythm thing between the art school rock sensibility and samples and great funk. Well, I sort of fell a cootie, King Sonny O'Day in the releases. And it was a great collection. Adrian Ballou on guitar, Stephen Scales, Noonan Hendrix, Dillette MacDonald. I think there's many more that just seem to feature a huge sort of two-band line-up happening, and this one is called the Jezebel Spirit."}, {"start": "01:11:12", "end": "01:12:29", "text": "You're a very, very, very talented man. The bass there by Byron Miller. George Duke was the writer and the producer. And, of course, Michael Cimbello, who became famous himself with rhythm guitar. Leon Chancellor on drums, who worked with Michael Jackson. Jerry Hay, who worked with everybody when it came to sort of horn arrangements over the years. That's Shine on the very top there. We had Brian Eno and David Byrne. My Life in the Bush of Ghost, 1981, I think it came out. It was recorded at 79, but it was delayed because of sample clearance, including that one there, which was just a found sample. of an exorcist reciting to the Jezebel spirit. That's the title of the tune. And, of course, never ask for any cut of the action, said reverend or exorcist, which I think is hilarious. This is sold out every week from 5 to 7. It is 6.11 right now. So I thought I'd play a track. This woman, I mean, I love this tune so much. I play tunes at a frequent sort of interval just to sort of remind people how good they are. Martha Wash was part of Two Tons of Fun. Two Tons of Fun, of course, with the backing singers with Sylvester, so way back in the day, and then she became the unknown voice and then known for her sample and then, of course, paid for for Black Box many, many years later, and this track is called Carry On and an incredible gospel singer."}, {"start": "01:19:48", "end": "01:20:41", "text": "Three great singers, Athena Johnson and Teresa Mowry and Latre Rahman, otherwise known as Dames Brown, one of the greatest names in the popular music and that is actually a brand new release. Yes indeed, Dames Brown, the brand new sort of album. That track there was called Who Do You Think You Are? And before that we had Martha Walsh and she knows who she is very much so carry on from the 1992 debut release under the title Martha Walsh and she was once in Two Tons of Fun, another great title, another great artist name. And then went on to Black Box, but solo album. That was her debut release. I think it sort of really kicked in 1993. Doing a lot of tracks from the 90s today, which is not a bad thing, including this next one from Ronnie Jordan, who I saw play at Kinsella's on the top floor back in the day. Jazz fusion with beats. Blues grinder is the tune from the album The Antidote, 1990."}, {"start": "01:33:49", "end": "01:35:13", "text": "If you didn't know any better, you'd swear it was Maceo Parker on saxophone. T